How steamcards farms trading cards follows ArchiSteamFarm
(https://github.com/JustArchiNET/ArchiSteamFarm), Copyright 2015-2026
Łukasz "JustArchi" Domeradzki, licensed under the Apache License 2.0:
the badge-page selectors (component/game/data/src/badge_page.rs and
library/steam-api/src/badges.rs), what an inventory item's tags say it is
(library/steam-api/src/inventory.rs), the farming rules and timings
(component/farming/domain/src/model/rules.rs), including its list of
sale-event app IDs, which licences are purchases Steam refunds
(library/steam-api/src/licences.rs), and the hours its time to finish adds
for games short of their hours (component/session/domain/src/model/forecast.rs).
They were rewritten in Rust for steamcards; no ASF source code is included.

Steam's message definitions follow Valve's own .proto files, as published by
SteamDatabase (https://github.com/SteamDatabase/Protobufs).

The market's currencies, how amounts are written in them, and Steam's fee
rules (component/money/domain/src/model/currency.rs and
component/account/domain/src/model/wallet.rs) follow Valve's own scripts for
steamcommunity.com, global.js and economy_common.js, as published by
SteamTracking (https://github.com/SteamTracking/SteamTracking). They were
rewritten in Rust for steamcards.
